                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stack"} derived stack
                  %h2#theorems Theorems
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu%27s+theorem"} Diaconescu's theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr%27s+theorem"} Barr's theorem
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/topos+theory+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#characterization"} Characterization
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#extensivity_and_exactness"} Extensivity and exactness
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#internal_logic"} Internal logic
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concept"} Related concept
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A quasitopos is a particular kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            that has properties similar to that of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/balanced+category"} balanced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            that is both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monic
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epic
            is not necessarily invertible. A quasitopos that is balanced is a topos.
          %p
            Instead of the usual
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monomorphism"} strong
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
            A2.6).
          %p
            Note that some of the literature definitions use the notion of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+monomorphism"} regular monomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monomorphism"} strong monomorphism
          %h2#definition Definition
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong quasitopos
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finitely complete
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+cocomplete+category"} finitely cocomplete
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              in which there exists an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              that classifies
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monomorphism"} strong monomorphisms
          %p In particular, this means
          %ul
            %li
              Every finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
              exists;
            %li
              For each morphism
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to B
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} pullback functor
              between
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+category"} slice quasitoposes
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*: E/B \to E/A,
              %p
                admits a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} right adjoint
            %li
              There is a map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t: 1 \to \Omega
              such that every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monomorphism"} strong monomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: A \to X
              occurs as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
              along some unique morphism
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi χ
                        %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_i: X \to \Omega
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi A
                            %mtd
                              %mo →
                            %mtd
                              %mn 1
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi i
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi t
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi χ
                                    %mi i
                            %mtd
                              %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      A &amp; \to &amp; 1\\
                      i \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow t\\
                      X &amp; \overset{\chi_i}{\to} &amp; \Omega
                      }
          %p
            The object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            above is sometimes called a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong strong-subobject classifier
            = succeed "," do
              %strong weak subobject classifier
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Equivalently, in addition to finite limits and colimits and local cartesian closure, one may ask only that there exists a classifier
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\colon 1\to\Omega
              as above for
              %em some
              class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
              which contains the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+monomorphisms"} regular monomorphisms
              and is closed under composition and pullback. From this one can show that every morphism factors as an epimorphism followed by a regular monomorphism (see
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#Wyler"} Wyler, proposition 12.5
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              is precisely the class of strong monomorphisms.
          .un_prop
            %h6#definitionproposition Definition/Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a category with two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topologies"} Grothendieck topologies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J\subseteq K
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi BiSep
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi J
                    %mo ,
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi PSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BiSep(C,J,K) \hookrightarrow PSh(C)
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              consisting of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              that are also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+presheaves"} separated
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              is a quasitopos. A category equivalent to such a category is called a
              = succeed "," do
                %strong Grothendieck quasitopos
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
          %p
            In particular, this includes the category of separated presheaves on a given site (if we take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            to be the trivial topology), and also includes all Grothendieck toposes (if we take
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo =
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K=J
          %p
            Equivalently, a Grothendieck quasitopos is a category of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Sep
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sep_k(\mathbf{E})
            = succeed "-separated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+topology"} Lawvere-Tierney topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            on a Grothendieck topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#general General
          #PushoutOfStrongMonos.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              %strong (pushout of strong monos)
            %p
              In a quasitopos the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monomorphism"} strong monomorphism
              is again a strong mono, and the resulting square is also a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              square.
          %p
            This appears as
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Elephant"} Elephant, Lemma A.2.6.2
            %em cocover
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %em strong monomorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            is a quasitopos in which all monomorphisms are strong, this implies that the pushout of a mono in a topos is again a mono and that the resulting square is a pullback. Together with the fact that colimits are universal in a topos, this implies that a topos is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adhesive+category"} adhesive category
          .num_corollary
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              A quasitopos that is also a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/balanced+category"} balanced category
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %p
            This is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Elephant"} Elephant, corollary 2.6.3
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              A quasitopos has
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+coproduct"} disjoint coproduct
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo →
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \to *
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monomorphism"} strong monomorphism
          %p
            This is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Elephant"} Elephant, corollary 2.6.5
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              An
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              in a quasitopos is called
              %strong coarse
              if for every
              %span.newWikiWord
                monic epic
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/bimorphism"} ?
              morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to B
              every morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to C
              factors uniquely through
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          %p
            So the coarse objects are those that see monic epic morphisms as
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/balanced+category"} balanced category
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              In a quasitopos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              on coarse objects is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Coarse
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mo ←
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Coarse(\mathcal{E}) \stackrel{\leftarrow}{\hookrightarrow} \mathcal{E}
                    \\,.
          %p
            This is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Elephant"} Elephant, prop 2.6.12
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_3 Lemma
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi SepPSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E} \simeq SepPSh(C)
              is a Grothendieck quasitopos of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+presheaves"} separated presheaves
              on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Coarse
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Coarse(\mathcal{E}) \simeq Sh(C)
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            This is in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Elephant"} Elephant, section A4.4
          %h3#characterization Characterization
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud+theorem"} Giraud theorem
            characterizing Grothendieck quasitoposes:
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Grothendieck quasitoposes are those quasitoposes which are
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generating+set"} generating set
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+categories"} locally presentable categories
              which are locally cartesian closed and in which every
              %em strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruence"} congruence
              has a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quotient"} effective quotient
          %p
            see C2.2.13 of the (
            %a{:href => "#Elephant"}> Elephant
            )
          %h3#extensivity_and_exactness Extensivity and exactness
          %p
            A topos is always
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact
          %p
            A quasitopos is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} coherent category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+initial+object"} strict
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                          %mo +
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{P &amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; B\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                  A &amp; \underset{}{\to} &amp; A+B}
          %p
            the object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            need not be initial. This is easy to see from the fact that any Heyting category is a quasitopos, since then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo +
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A+B
            is the join
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\vee B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\wedge B
          %p
            It is true, however, that such a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is always a
            %em quotient
            of the initial object, i.e. the unique map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\to P
            is epic. If the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\to 1
            is strong monic, as it is in the “topological” examples, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            can have no proper epimorphic images, and so coproducts are disjoint. The converse also holds, since if coproducts are disjoint then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\to 1
            is an equalizer of the two injections
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\rightrightarrows 1+1
            = succeed "." do
              %strong solid
          %p
            More generally, in any quasitopos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\to 1
            into an epic followed by a strong monic,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\to \bar{0} \to 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mover
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E/\bar{0}
            is a Heyting algebra (i.e. a posetal quasitopos), while the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-slice+category"} co-slice category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{0}/E
            is a solid quasitopos, and moreover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            itself is recoverable via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+gluing"} Artin gluing
            from a particular functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mover
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E/\bar{0} \to \bar{0}/E
          %p
            By contrast, if a solid quasitopos is additionally
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/balanced+category"} balanced
            = succeed "," do
              %em quasi-exact
            %em strong
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruence"} congruence
            has an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quotient"} effective quotient
          %h3#internal_logic Internal logic
          %p
            Like a topos, a quasitopos has an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            %em strong
            subobjects. The resulting internal logic fails to satisfy the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+comprehension"} function comprehension principle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctions"} anafunctions
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Any (elementary)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                is a quasitopos. The first two properties are known, and in a topos every monomorphism is strong, so the ordinary subobject classifier works.
              %p
                Conversely, if a quasitopos is also a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/balanced+category"} balanced category
            %li
              %p
                Any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
                is a quasitopos. This is in notable contrast to the case of topoi, since no nontrivial poset is a topos. The crucial distinction is that every morphism in a poset is both monic and epic, but only the identities are strong monic or strong epic.
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudotopological+spaces"} pseudotopological spaces
                is a quasitopos, as is the category of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsequential+spaces"} subsequential spaces
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
                fails only to be locally cartesian closed. In such “topological” quasitopoi, the strong monics are the “subspace inclusions” (i.e. those monics whose source has the topology induced from the target), and the strong-subobject classifier is the two-point space with the indiscrete topology. (In particular, we cannot demand any sort of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axiom"} separation axiom
                and still have a quasitopos.)
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/marked+simplicial+set"} marked simplicial set
            %li
              %p
                A category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+sheaves"} concrete sheaves
                on a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+site"} concrete site
                is a Grothendieck quasitopos. See
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+topos"} local topos
              %p This includes the following examples:
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    The category of
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+complex"} simplicial complexes
                %li
                  %p
                    The category of
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological spaces
            %li#exampsep
              %p
                The following examples are categories of separated presheaves for the
                = succeed "-topology" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
              %ul
                %li#mono
                  %p
                    The category
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-category#def"} Mono
                    of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
                    between sets (as presheaves on the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+category"} interval category
                    \- the
                    = succeed ")." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sierpinski+topos"} Sierpinski topos
                %li#endorel
                  %p
                    The category
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation#endorel"} EndoRel or Bin
                    of sets equipped with a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
                    (as presheaves on
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quiv"} Quiv
                  .maruku-equation
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mn 1
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 0
                          %mover
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mi t
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi s
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_1 = (0 \stackrel{\overset{s}{\to}}{\underset{t}{\to}} 1),
                  %p
                    a truncation of the
                    = succeed ")." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globular+category"} globular category
                %li#endoref
                  %p The category of sets equipped with a reflexive relation (as presheaves on a truncated reflexive globular category).
                %li#endosym
                  %p
                    The category of sets equipped with a symmetric relation (as presheaves on the full subcategory of finite sets and injections consisting of just the objects
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mn 1
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                    = succeed ")." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
                %li#endorefsym
                  %p
                    The category of sets equipped with a reflexive symmetric relation (as presheaves on the full subcategory of finite sets consisting of just the objects
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mn 1
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                    = succeed ")." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+simple+graphs"} category of simple graphs
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bornological+set"} bornological sets
            %li
              %p
                The category of assemblies of a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+combinatory+algebra"} partial combinatory algebra
            %li
              %p
                The category of Spanier’s
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-topological+space"} quasi-topological spaces
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#DE"} Dubuc-Espanol
          %h2#related_concept Related concept
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong quasitopos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-quasitopos"} (∞,1)-quasitopos
          %h2#references References
          %p Original articles include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacques+Penon"}> Jacques Penon
                ,
                %em Quasi-topos
                , C.R. Acad. Sci. Paris
                %strong 276
                Série A (1973) pp.237–240. (
                %a{:href => "http://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/bpt6k6217213f/f251.image"}> gallica
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacques+Penon"}> Jacques Penon
                ,
                %em Sur le quasi-topos
                , Cahiers Top. Géom. Diff. 18 (1977), 181–218.
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#Wyler
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oswald+Wyler"}> Oswald Wyler
                ,
                %em Lecture Notes on Topoi and Quasitopoi
                , World Scientific Singapore 1991 (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/1047"}> doi:10.1142/1047
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
              %p#Elephant (section A2.6)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Ad%C3%A1mek"}> Jiří Adámek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Horst+Herrlich"} Horst Herrlich
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Strecker"} George Strecker
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Abstract and Concrete Categories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/17/tr17abs.html"} tac:tr17
          %p
            Quasi-toposes of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+sheaves"} concrete sheaves
            are considered in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduardo+Dubuc"}> Eduardo Dubuc
                ,
                %em Concrete quasitopoi
                , Lecture Notes in Math. 753 (1979), 239–254
            %li#DE
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduardo+Dubuc"}> Eduardo Dubuc
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luis+Espa%C3%B1ol"} Luis Español
                %em Quasitopoi over a base category
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.CT/0612727"}> arXiv:math.CT/0612727
                )
          %p A review is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Hoffnung"} Alex Hoffnung
              = succeed "," do
                %em Convenient categories of smooth spaces
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0807.1704"} arXiv
          %p
            More generally, quasi-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+toposes"} sheaf toposes
            are discussed in
          %ul#GarnerLack
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"}> Richard Garner
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Lack"} Stephen Lack
              %em Grothendieck quasitoposes
              , arXiv:1106.5331 (2012). (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1106.5331"}> abstract
              )
